WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Part 4 You will hear part of an Environmental Studies

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.920
lecture on tree planting. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Part 5 You will hear part of an Environmental Studies lecture on tree planting. First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:11.720
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.800
Tree planting now dominates political and popular agendas, and is often presented as

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:24.160
an easy answer to the climate crisis, as well as a way for business corporations to offset

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.160
their carbon emissions. But unfortunately, tree planting isn't as straightforward as

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.680
some people think. When the wrong trees are planted in the wrong place, it can do considerably

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:44.519
more damage than good, failing to help either people or the environment.

00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:50.480
Reforestation projects are currently being undertaken on a huge scale in many countries,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.540
but it's crucial that the right trees are selected. A mix of species should always

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:03.919
be planted, typical of the local natural forest ecosystem, and including rare and endangered

00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:11.679
species in order to create a rich ecosystem. It's important to avoid non-native species

00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:18.500
that could become invasive. Invasive species are a significant contributor to the current

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:25.880
global biodiversity crisis, and are often in competition with native species and may

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:34.979
threaten their long-term survival. Restoring biodiversity that will maximise carbon capture

00:02:34.979 --> 00:02:42.899
is key when reforesting an area, but ideally any reforestation project should have several

00:02:42.899 --> 00:02:50.500
goals. These could include selecting trees that can contribute to wildlife conservation,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.660
improve the availability of food for the local community, and maintain the stability of soil

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:05.660
systems. Meeting as many of these goals as possible, whilst doing no harm to local communities,

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:12.460
native ecosystems and vulnerable species, is the sign of a highly successful tree planting

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:20.460
scheme. To ensure the survival and resilience of a planted forest, it's vital to use tree

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:28.380
seeds with appropriate levels of genetic diversity, the amount of genetic variation found within

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:36.580
a species essential for their survival. Using seeds with low genetic diversity generally

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:44.419
lowers the resilience of restored forests, which can make them vulnerable to disease and unable to

00:03:44.419 --> 00:03:52.339
adapt to climate change. Choosing the right location for reforestation projects is as

00:03:52.339 --> 00:04:00.500
important as choosing the right trees. Ultimately, the best area for planting trees would be in

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:09.059
formerly forested areas that are in poor condition. It's better to avoid non-forested landscapes,

00:04:09.059 --> 00:04:17.540
such as natural grasslands, savannas or wetlands, as these ecosystems already contribute greatly

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:24.899
to capturing carbon. It would also be advantageous to choose an area where trees could provide

00:04:24.899 --> 00:04:33.059
other benefits, such as recreational spaces. Reforesting areas which are currently exploited

00:04:33.059 --> 00:04:40.019
for agriculture should be avoided, as this often leads to other areas being deforested.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:52.579
Large-scale reforestation projects require careful planning. Making the right decisions

00:04:52.579 --> 00:05:01.220
about where to plant trees depends on having the right information. Having detailed and up-to-date

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:09.700
maps identifying high-priority areas for intervention is essential. Drone technology

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:17.380
is a useful tool in helping to prioritise and monitor areas of degraded forest for restoration.

00:05:18.179 --> 00:05:25.380
In Brazil, it's being used to identify and quantify how parts of the Amazon are being

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:32.100
devastated by human activities, such as rearing cattle and illegal logging.

00:05:33.779 --> 00:05:39.140
A good example of where the right trees were picked to achieve a restored forest

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:47.220
is in Lampang province in northern Thailand. A previously forested site which had been degraded

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:54.420
through mining was reforested by a cement company, together with Chiang Mai University.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:03.859
After spreading 60 centimetres of topsoil, they planted 14 different native tree species,

00:06:03.859 --> 00:06:12.339
which included several species of fig. Figs are a keystone species because of the critical role

00:06:12.339 --> 00:06:20.660
they play in maintaining wildlife populations. They are central to tropical reforestation projects

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:28.019
as they accelerate the speed of the recovery process by attracting animals and birds which

00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:36.260
act as natural seed dispersers. This helps to promote diversity through the healthy regrowth

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:44.739
of a wide range of plant species. Unlike the majority of fruit trees, figs bear fruit all year

00:06:44.739 --> 00:06:53.220
round, providing a reliable food source for many species. At this site for example, after only three

00:06:53.220 --> 00:07:00.980
rainy seasons, monkeys started visiting to eat the fig fruits, naturally dispersing seeds through

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:08.899
defecation. Reforestation projects should always aim to make sure that local communities are

00:07:08.899 --> 00:07:16.660
consulted and involved in the decision-making process. The restoration of mangrove forests

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:23.940
in Madagascar is an example of a project which has succeeded in creating real benefits for the community.

00:07:24.899 --> 00:07:33.299
Destruction of the mangrove forests had a terrible impact on plant and animal life and also badly

00:07:33.299 --> 00:07:39.859
affected the fishing industry, which was a major source of employment for local people living in

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:49.299
coastal areas. The reforestation project involved hiring local people to plant and care for the new

00:07:49.299 --> 00:07:57.059
mangrove trees. Millions of mangrove trees have now been planted, which has resulted in the return

00:07:57.059 --> 00:08:05.299
of a healthy aquatic ecosystem. The mangroves also act as a defence against the increased threat of

00:08:05.299 --> 00:08:13.619
flooding caused by climate change. What's more, the local economy is more stable and thousands

00:08:13.619 --> 00:08:21.700
more Madagascans are now able to send their children to school. One other important point to consider...

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:32.500
That is the end of part four. You now have one minute to check your answers to part four.

